> BTW, the "AoESP entry in the Wiki does not show probabilities. Why?

ESP is special because it can be reliably tested for in all cases,
without resources, instantly, and even before curse-testing it.

It is necessary to test an uncursed amulet even for the harmful types
(and optionally cursed ones for good types) simply because they can be
of that type. All other tests require some work: getting it out of the
shop if necessary, testing for curses, then finding pools, or finding
a ray type wand, eating a mimic, waiting 100 turns in a safe spot
unless sleep resistant,  poisoning yourself unless resistant, and so
on. In the testing process, the probabilities are a guide what to test
for first, and what to bank on if you have to gamble. In this
situation, it is already known whether the amulet is ESP.

Formally, the displayed percentages are the conditional probabilities
for the various types if the amulet's curse status is known and if it
can be artifact-named.

Of course, it is possible to compute the conditional probabilites on
the curse status alone. An anonymous wiki editor has even done that.
However, those are not the numbers that are most useful in real games,
as played by the majority of all players.

Please put any follow-ups regarding the wiki entry on
http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Talk:Amulet .

> [*] I am sure there are folks who don't want to solve situations by
